cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this analysis or its reproducibility code, cite the version or
  commit used and the underlying papers and datasets relevant to your work.
  See docs/references.bib and ACKNOWLEDGMENTS.md for original-source credits.
title: 'Wish You Were Green: white-dwarf chromaticity research'
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Moriwaki
    given-names: Shota
version: 0.2.0
date-released: '2026-10-18'
url: https://github.com/dj-thank/wish-you-were-green
repository-code: https://github.com/dj-thank/wish-you-were-green
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Public-spectrum analysis of white-dwarf chromaticity, with an audit of
  spectral flux conventions, corrected candidate screening, native Gaia XP
  covariance propagation, photometric calibration stress tests, public
  time-series and additional-epoch observations, and source-identity checks.
  Version 0.2 corrects mixed Fnu and Flambda interpretations in archived SDSS
  spectra and withdraws independent SDSS colour corroboration for WD
  J1323-0129. Historical version 0.1 numerical products remain identifiable.
  This is exploratory, unreviewed research with ChatGPT-assisted analysis;
  it does not establish confirmed discoveries, complete uncertainty budgets,
  worldwide priority or new atmospheric physics. Project initiator and
  maintainer: Shota Moriwaki. Original code is Apache-2.0, original prose is
  CC BY 4.0, and third-party material retains its original terms.
keywords:
  - astronomy
  - white dwarfs
  - public spectra
  - chromaticity
  - CIE 1931
  - Gaia
  - DESI
  - reproducible research
preferred-citation:
  type: report
  authors:
    - family-names: Moriwaki
      given-names: Shota
  title: 'Wish You Were Green: white-dwarf chromaticity research'
  year: 2026
